<section class="rw-hero"><div class="rw-in rw-c"><p class="rw-kick">An independent guide to reading well</p><h1>Read more. <em>Remember more of it.</em></h1>
<p class="rw-lead">Readwise is a study site about one skill: reading. Find out how long a book will really take, set a goal you can keep, test your reading speed, and learn the habits that make what you read stay with you.</p>
<p class="rw-acts"><a class="btn" href="#time">How long will my book take?</a><a class="rw-link" href="active-reading-guide.html">Learn active reading</a></p></div>
<div class="rw-in"><figure class="rw-hero-f"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1762803842029-5e34663d98be?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1400&h=620&q=75" alt="woman reading a book in a modern armchair" width="1400" height="620" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption><b>Fifteen minutes a day</b> is enough to finish a book a month.</figcaption></figure></div></section>

<section class="rw-nums" aria-label="Reading in numbers"><div class="rw-in rw-nums-g"><div><b>240</b><span>words a minute is a typical adult pace for everyday prose</span></div><div><b>10</b><span>pages a day finishes a 300-page book every month</span></div><div><b>50</b><span>pages is a fair trial before putting a book down</span></div><div><b>1</b><span>line of notes per chapter is all a reading log needs</span></div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk" id="time"><div class="rw-in"><div class="rw-feat"><div class="rw-feat-a"><p class="rw-kick">Tool one</p><h2>Reading Time Calculator</h2><p>Enter the length of the book and how long you read each day. The calculator works out the total hours and the date you will turn the last page.</p>
<div class="rw-fields"><label for="rt-p">Pages<input id="rt-p" type="number" min="1" max="3000" value="320" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="rt-k">Kind of book<select id="rt-k"><option value="180">Children&rsquo;s or large print</option><option value="275" selected>Standard paperback</option><option value="380">Dense textbook or academic</option></select></label>
<label for="rt-s">Your pace<select id="rt-s"><option value="160">Careful (160 wpm)</option><option value="240" selected>Typical (240 wpm)</option><option value="320">Brisk (320 wpm)</option></select></label><label for="rt-m">Minutes a day<input id="rt-m" type="number" min="5" max="600" value="25" inputmode="numeric"></label></div></div>
<div class="rw-feat-b" id="rt-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk rw-alt"><div class="rw-in"><p class="rw-kick">Thirty good minutes</p><h2>How a reading session should go</h2><ol class="rw-line">
<li><span>0:00</span><h3>Put the phone in another room</h3><p>Not face down on the table. Out of reach. This single step does more than any technique.</p></li>
<li><span>0:01</span><h3>Recall where you were</h3><p>Before opening the book, say in one sentence what happened last time. It warms up your memory.</p></li>
<li><span>0:02</span><h3>Read with a pencil nearby</h3><p>Mark a passage only when it surprises you or answers a question you had.</p></li>
<li><span>0:27</span><h3>Stop mid-chapter if you like</h3><p>Stopping at an interesting point makes it far easier to pick the book up tomorrow.</p></li>
<li><span>0:28</span><h3>Write one line</h3><p>Close the book and write a single sentence about what you just read. That is the whole log.</p></li></ol></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk"><div class="rw-in"><p class="rw-kick">Tools two and three</p><h2>Set a goal, then check your pace</h2><div class="rw-two">
<div class="rw-card" id="goal"><h3>Reading Goal Planner</h3><p>Turn a yearly target into a daily number of pages.</p><div class="rw-fields"><label for="rg-b">Books this year<input id="rg-b" type="number" min="1" max="200" value="18" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="rg-p">Average length (pages)<input id="rg-p" type="number" min="50" max="1500" value="300" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="rg-d">Reading days a week<select id="rg-d"><option>3</option><option>4</option><option selected>5</option><option>6</option><option>7</option></select></label></div><div class="rw-res" id="rg-r" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="rw-card" id="speed"><h3>Reading Speed Test</h3><p>Press start, read the passage at your normal pace, then press the button again.</p><div class="rw-pass" id="rs-t" data-state="idle"><p>Every town used to have a small library, and ours stood at the end of a street lined with lime trees. It was not a grand building. The roof leaked in one corner, and the heating clanked whenever it was switched on. Yet on Saturday mornings the place was full. Children sat cross-legged on the carpet, turning pages with great seriousness. Older readers claimed the armchairs by the window and guarded them politely. The librarian knew what almost everyone liked and kept a pile of suggestions behind the desk. If you returned a book and said you had enjoyed it, she would nod, reach down, and hand you another without a word. Nobody rushed. People read a chapter, looked out at the trees for a while, and read another. I learned there that reading is not a race. It is closer to a long walk, taken at whatever pace lets you notice things along the way.</p></div><p><button class="btn" id="rs-b" type="button">Start reading</button></p><div class="rw-res" id="rs-r" aria-live="polite"><p>Your result will appear here. Read to understand, not to win.</p></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk rw-ink"><div class="rw-in"><p class="rw-kick">The two guides</p><h2>Start with whichever problem is yours</h2><div class="rw-guides">
<a class="rw-g" href="reading-habit-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769491277413-53e94ff305fc?auto=format&fit=crop&w=680&h=400&q=75" alt="person reading a book with coffee at a table" width="680" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><span>If you do not read enough</span><h3>The Reading Habit Guide</h3><p>Choosing a time, choosing the right book and getting through the weeks when you would rather scroll.</p></div></a>
<a class="rw-g" href="active-reading-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769794371007-9bf419217727?auto=format&fit=crop&w=680&h=400&q=75" alt="open notebook filled with handwritten notes" width="680" height="400" loading="lazy"><div><span>If you forget what you read</span><h3>The Active Reading Guide</h3><p>Previewing, questioning, margin notes and a five-minute review routine that makes ideas stay.</p></div></a>
</div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk"><div class="rw-in"><p class="rw-kick">Paper, screen or headphones</p><h2>Formats compared</h2><div class="tblw"><table class="rw-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Best for</th><th>Note-taking</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Printed book</td><td>Study, long sessions, anything you will return to</td><td>Pencil in the margin; easy to flick back</td><td>Weight in a bag; needs good light</td></tr>
<tr><td>E-reader</td><td>Travel, reading in bed, large-print needs</td><td>Highlights are quick and searchable</td><td>Highlighting everything and reviewing nothing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phone or tablet</td><td>Ten spare minutes in a queue</td><td>Possible, but fiddly</td><td>Notifications; switch them off first</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audiobook</td><td>Stories, memoir, walks and chores</td><td>Voice memo or a note afterwards</td><td>Attention drifting during complex passages</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk rw-alt"><div class="rw-in"><p class="rw-kick">Worth unlearning</p><h2>Three reading myths</h2><div class="rw-myths">
<div><h3><s>Fast readers are better readers</s></h3><p>Good readers change speed constantly. They glide through the familiar and slow right down for the difficult. A single high number on a speed test says little about understanding.</p></div>
<div><h3><s>You must finish every book</s></h3><p>There are more good books than anyone can read in a lifetime. Time spent pushing through one you dislike is time taken from one you would have loved.</p></div>
<div><h3><s>Rereading is the best way to learn</s></h3><p>Rereading feels productive because the page looks familiar. Closing the book and trying to recall the argument is harder, and it works far better.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk"><div class="rw-in rw-corner"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758024836397-2c9c698087f0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&h=760&q=75" alt="cozy armchair by a window with a garden view" width="700" height="760" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="rw-kick">Set the scene</p><h2>Build a reading corner</h2><p>People read more when there is a place that is obviously for reading. It does not need to be a room. A chair, a light and a surface will do.</p><ul class="rw-ticks"><li>A chair you can sit in for half an hour without shifting</li><li>Light that comes from beside or behind you, not from above</li><li>A small table for the book, a pencil and a drink</li><li>The current book left there, open side down, in plain sight</li><li>No charger within reach</li></ul></div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk rw-alt"><div class="rw-in"><p class="rw-kick">Never be without the next one</p><h2>Keep three shelves</h2><div class="rw-shelves"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1731983568657-84b7a4f61e27?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=380&q=75" alt="stack of books on a table" width="520" height="380" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Now.</b> One main book, and at most one lighter book for tired evenings.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1778088442532-2a3ed3767931?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=380&q=75" alt="bookshelves filled with books" width="520" height="380" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Next.</b> Three to five books you already own or have reserved, so finishing one never leaves a gap.</figcaption></figure><div class="rw-shelf-t"><h3>Someday.</h3><p>A running list of everything recommended to you, kept in a notebook or a notes app. When the Next shelf runs low, promote from here. The list is allowed to be long; the Next shelf is not.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk"><div class="rw-in rw-faqg"><div><p class="rw-kick">Asked often</p><h2>Reading questions, answered plainly</h2></div><div class="rw-faq"><details open><summary>What is a normal reading speed?</summary><p>Most adults read ordinary prose silently at somewhere between 200 and 300 words a minute, with around 240 as a typical figure. Dense textbooks and unfamiliar subjects are slower, and that is expected rather than a problem.</p></details><details><summary>Is speed reading worth learning?</summary><p>Skimming is a useful skill for deciding what deserves a careful read. Claims of reading whole books at several times normal speed with full understanding do not hold up; past a certain pace, comprehension drops. Reading more often beats reading faster.</p></details><details><summary>How many pages a day do I need to read a book a month?</summary><p>A typical 300-page book works out at ten pages a day. For most readers that is fifteen to twenty minutes, which is why a short daily session is enough to finish twelve books a year.</p></details><details><summary>Should I finish a book I am not enjoying?</summary><p>Not out of duty. Give it a fair start, about fifty pages, and if it still is not working, put it down and pick up another. Readers who allow themselves to stop tend to read far more over a year.</p></details><details><summary>Do audiobooks count as reading?</summary><p>For stories and general non-fiction, understanding from listening is very close to understanding from print. For material you need to study, print or an e-reader makes it easier to pause, reread and take notes.</p></details><details><summary>How do I remember what I read?</summary><p>Do something with it. Close the book and say the main point in your own words, write one line per chapter, and look back at those lines a few days later. The Active Reading Guide sets out the whole routine.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="rw-blk rw-ink rw-brief"><div class="rw-in rw-c"><h2>The Readwise Brief</h2><p>One reading technique and one book-choosing idea, sent on the first Sunday of each month.</p><p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@readwise.com?subject=The%20Readwise%20Brief&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></p><small>No tracking links. Unsubscribe with one reply.</small></div></section>

